    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.notify\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.12 (!at least latest Nightly build!)\n\n### Description\n\nWhile notifications do the work, operator mode keymaps (at least those provided by `mini.jump`) do not work reliably. This becomes an issue when using `lua-language-server` with `mini.notify`, as LSP indexing can take some time, making operator keymaps unreliable.\n\nIf I remember correctly, this issue does not occur when using [fidget.nvim](https://github.com/j-hui/fidget.nvim).\n\nIf possible, this should be addressed in the modules where the keymaps are defined (such as `mini.jump`, `mini.ai`) or even in the Neovim itself. However, a practical workaround would be to stop `mini.notify` while an operator keymap is active (for example, by checking `vim.v.operator`).\n\nI originally encountered this while using [LSP progress without plugins](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/discussions/35349), but since the issue also seems to occur with `mini.notify`, I'm reporting it here.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> The repro is bad since `defer_fn` does the work, but the issue should still be valid.\n\n```lua\nif vim.fn.has(\"nvim-0.12\") == 0 then\n  return vim.notify(\"Install Neovim 0.12+\", vim.log.levels.ERROR)\nend\n\nvim.pack.add({\n  { src = \"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim\" },\n})\n\nrequire(\"mini.notify\").setup()\nrequire(\"mini.ai\").setup()\nrequire(\"mini.jump\").setup()\n\nlocal function lsp_report()\n  vim.defer_fn(function()\n    vim.notify(\"Unbelievable message\")\n    lsp_report()\n  end, 100)\nend\n\nlsp_report()\n```\n\n```\nnvim --noplugin init.lua\n4j\ndf(\ndi + wait + f\n```\n\n*Try using operator keymaps multiple times, or lower the `lsp_report` timeout value.*",
