        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This should now be resolved on the latest `main`.\n\nThe issue was not *quite* Operator-pending mode mappings, but expression mappings. It just so happened that 'mini.ai', 'mini.jump' (and some others) modules use expression mappings in Operator-pending mode (for dot-repeat).\n\nThe issue is that changing text in other buffers when expression mapping is active is not allowed (`:h :map-expression`), but 'mini.notify' tries to do so. This resulted in an error for `vim.fn.getcharstr()` used in those expression mappings to get the key, which was treated by their code as cancellation of the action (similar to how `<C-c>` causes error).\n\nIt now should be resolved by delaying notification window refresh until after it is safe to do so. Unfortunately, there is no built-in reliable way to check if it is safe to set buffer text (see neovim/neovim#38034), so manually checking for the error while setting a text for a dummy buffer is the only good way here. This should not add too much overhead since the buffer is cached.\n\nThanks again for the issue. I realized that I had noticed similar problems in the past, just not frequent enough to actually remember to dig deeper. Finding that it is 'mini.notify' notifications are the problem was really helpful!",
